20636 0 obj
<</Alt(EternalBlue\rIn 2017, a serious vulnerability called EternalBlue was found in SMB Version 1 \(SMBv1\).\r\rThe vulnerability allowed an attacker to install malware on any computer running SMB1, without any action required by the user.\r\rMicrosoft released an emergency patch \(MS17-010\) for this vulnerability that covered all supported Windows versions.\rWannaCry\rWannaCry was an attack that leveraged the EternalBlue vulnerability. \r\rIt spread very quickly, destroying compromised systems. \r\rIf SMB1 is enabled on a system, WannaCry can use it without any user intervention, install ransomware payloads, and then scan and infect other SMB1 compatible systems connected to the infected system.\r\rWannaCry caused significant damage for governments, institutions and companies from the medical, automotive, communications, transportation and other industries. Microsoft took an unprecedented action and provided fixes for end-of-life versions of Windows, including Windows XP.\rNyetya\rNyetya was originally conceived as a supply chain attack, and was also distributed via EternalBlue. \r\rIt also took advantage of another SMB vulnerability called EternalRomance, which was very effective in old Windows versions. Nyetya appeared to be Ransomware, but in fact it was wipeware. \r\rIt displayed a Ransomware message, but users couldn't pay, and all data on infected systems was lost.\rOthers\rThere are additional scenarios in which attackers leverage the SMB protocol, even without relying on a vulnerability.\r\rThreats like Bad Rabbit, Olympic Destroyer and SamSam used various methods to gain access to a network, and once inside, used SMB to gain access to sensitive systems. \r\rIn other cases, attackers conducted brute force attacks on SMB shares, trying a large number of passwords until they gained access to sensitive data.\r)/K[ 20635 0 R  20637 0 R  20638 0 R  20639 0 R  20640 0 R  20641 0 R  20642 0 R  20643 0 R ]/P 20634 0 R /S/Diagram>>
endobj
